ponse\n\nsklearn/calibration.py\nline: 840-945\nfunction: calibration_curve\n\nsklearn/utils/__init__.py\nline: 1085-1102\nfunction: check_matplotlib_support\n\nsklearn/metrics/_plot/roc_curve.py\nline: 1-50\nfunction: RocCurveDisplay\n\nsklearn/calibration.py\nline: 1024-1078\nclass: CalibrationDisplay\n```\n\nExplanation of the locations:\n\n1. `sklearn/metrics/_plot/base.py`: This file contains the `_get_response` function that handles the common plotting logic and validation for metrics. Since our calibration curve plot will follow a similar pattern to other scikit-learn plots, we should use this file for implementing our plotting function.\n\n2. `sklearn/calibration.py`: The existing `calibration_curve` function is the core implementation that computes the calibration curve data points. This function will be used by our new plotting function to get the data points to plot.\n\n3. `sklearn/utils/__init__.py`: Contains the `check_matplotlib_support` function that ensures matplotlib is available before plotting. This is important for maintaining consistency with scikit-learn's plotting API.\n\n4. `sklearn/metrics/_plot/roc_curve.py`: This contains the `RocCurveDisplay` class which provides a reference for the plotting style and functionality. Our new function should follow a similar pattern to ensure consistency with scikit-learn's visualization standards.\n\n5. `sklearn/calibration.py`: The `CalibrationDisplay` class is the current visualization implementation in the calibration module, which we can use as a reference for implementing our new plotting function.\n\nThe reason for these specific locations:\n\n1. The plotting functionality should be implemented in in `sklearn/metrics/_plot/base.py` because it follows scikit-learn's existing plotting conventions and has the necessary infrastructure for matplotlib support.\n\n2. The calibration curve computation is already implemented in in `calibration.py`, so we don't need to duplicate that functionality.\n\n3. Using