he values as equally\n7669 |               spaced. This is the only method supported on MultiIndexes.\n7670 |             * 'time': Works on daily and higher resolution data to interpolate\n7671 |               given length of interval.\n7672 |             * 'index', 'values': use the actual numerical values of the index.\n7673 |             * 'nearest', 'zero', 'slinear', 'quadratic', 'cubic',\n7674 |               'barycentric', 'polynomial': Passed to\n7675 |               `scipy.interpolate.interp1d`, whereas 'spline' is passed to\n7676 |               `scipy.interpolate.UnivariateSpline`. These methods use the numerical\n7677 |               values of the index.  Both 'polynomial' and 'spline' require that\n7678 |               you also specify an `order` (int), e.g.\n7679 |               ``df.interpolate(method='polynomial', order=5)``. Note that,\n7680 |               `slinear` method in Pandas refers to the Scipy first order `spline`\n7681 |               instead of Pandas first order `spline`.\n7682 |             * 'krogh', 'piecewise_polynomial', 'spline', 'pchip', 'akima',\n7683 |               'cubicspline': Wrappers around the SciPy interpolation methods of\n7684 |               similar names. See `Notes`.\n7685 |             * 'from_derivatives': Refers to\n7686 |               `scipy.interpolate.BPoly.from_derivatives`.\n7687 | \n7688 |         axis : {{0 or 'index', 1 or 'columns', None}}, default None\n7689 |             Axis to interpolate along. For `Series` this parameter is unused\n7690 |             and defaults to 0.\n7691 |         limit : int, optional\n7692 |             Maximum number of consecutive NaNs to fill. Must be greater than\n7693 |             0.\n7694 |         inplace : bool, default False\n7695 |             Update the data in place if possible.\n7696 |         limit_direction : {{'forward', 'backward', 'both'}}, optional, default 'forward'\n7697 |             Consecutive NaNs will be filled in this direction.\n7698 | \n7699 |         limit_area : {{`None`, 'inside', 'outside'}}, default None\n7700 |             If limit is specified, consecutive NaNs will be filled with this\n7701 |             restriction.\n7702 | \n7703 |             * ``None``: No fill restriction.\n7704 |             * 'inside': Only fill NaNs surrounded by valid values\n7705 |               (interpolate).\n7706 |             * 'outside': Only fill NaNs outside valid values (extrapolate).\n7707 | \n7708 |         **kwargs : optional\n7709 |             Keyword arguments to pass on to the interpolating function.\n7710 | \n7711 |         Returns\n7712 |         -------\n7713 |         Series or DataFrame or None\n7714 |             Returns the same object type as the caller, interpolated at\n7715 |             some or all ``NaN`` values or None if ``inplace=True``.\n7716 | \n7717 |         See Also\n7718 |         --------\n7719 |         fillna : Fill missing values using different methods.\n7720 |         scipy.interpolate.Akima1DInterpolator : Piecewise cubic polynomials\n7721 |             (Akima interpolator).\n7722 |         scipy.interpolate.BPoly.from_derivatives : Piecewise polynomial in the\n7723 |             Bernstein basis.\n7724 |         scipy.interpolate.interp1d : Interpolate a 1-D function.\n7725 |         scipy.interpolate.KroghInterpolator : Interpolate polynomial (Krogh\n7726 |             interpolator).\n7727 |         scipy.interpolate.PchipInterpolator : PCHIP 1-d monotonic cubic\n7728 |             interpolation.\n7729 |         scipy.interpolate.CubicSpline : Cubic spline data interpolator.\n7730 | \n7731 |         Notes\n7732 |         -----\n7733 |         The 'krogh', 'piecewise_polynomial', 'spline', 'pchip' and 'akima'\n7734 |         methods are wrappers around the respective SciPy implementations of\n7735 |         similar names. These use the actual numerical values of the index.\n7736 |         For more information on their behavior, see the\n7737 |         `SciPy documentation\n7738 |         <https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/interpolate.html#univariate-interpolation>`__.\n7739 | \n7740 |         Examples\n7741 |         --------\n7742 |         Filling in ``NaN`` in a :class:`~pandas.Series` via linear\n7743 |         interpolation.\n7744 | \n7745 |         >>> s = pd.Series([0, 1, np.nan, 3])\n7746 |         >>> s\n7747 |         0    0.0\n7748 |         1    1.0\n7749 |         2    NaN\n7750 |         3    3.0\n7751 |         dtype: float64\n7752 |         >>> s.interpolate()\n7753 |         0    0.0\n7754 |         1    1.0\n7755 |         2    2.0\n7756 |         3    3.0\n7757 |         dtype: float64\n7758 | \n7759 |         Filling in ``NaN`` in a Series via polynomial interpolation or splines:\n7760 |         Both 'polynomial' and 'spline' methods require that you also specify\n7761 |         an ``order`` (int).\n7762 | \n7763 |         >>> s = pd.Series([0, 2, np.nan, 8])\n7764 |         >>> s.interpolate(method=\"polynomial\", order=2)\n7765 |         0    0.000000\n7766 |         1    2.000000\n7767 |         2    4.666667\n7768 |         3    8.000000\n7769 |         dtype: float64\n7770 | \n7771 |         Fill the DataFrame forward (that is, going down) along each column\n7772 |         using linear interpolation.\n7773 | \n7774 |         Note how the last entry in column 'a' is interpolated differently,\n7775 |         because there is no entry after it to use for interpolation.\n7776 |         Note how the first entry in column 'b' remains ``NaN``, because there\n7777 |         is no entry before it to use for interpolation.\n7778 | \n7779 |         >>> df = pd.DataFrame(\n7780 |         ...     [\n7781 |         ...         (0.0, np.nan, -1.0, 1.0),\n7782 |         ...         (np.nan, 2.0, np.nan, np.nan),\n7783 |         ...         (2.0, 3.0, np.nan, 9.0),\n7784 |         ...         (np.nan, 4.0, -4.0, 16.0),\n7785 |         ...     ],\n7786 |         ...     columns=list(\"abcd\"),\n7787 |         ... )\n7788 |         >>> df\n7789 |              a    b    c     d\n7790 |         0  0.0  NaN -1.0   1.0\n7791 |         1  NaN  2.0  NaN   NaN\n7792 |         2  2.0  3.0  NaN   9.0\n7793 |         3  NaN  4.0 -4.0  16.0\n7794 |         >>> df.interpolate(method=\"linear\", limit_direction=\"forward\", axis=0)\n7795 |              a    b    c     d\n7796 |         0  0.0  NaN -1.0   1.0\n7797 |         1  1.0  2.0 -2.0   5.0\n7798 |         2  2.0  3.0 -3.0   9.0\n7799 |         3  2.0  4.0 -4.0  16.0\n7800 | \n7801 |         Using polynomial interpolation.\n7802 | \n7803 |         >>> df[\"d\"].interpolate(method=\"polynomial\", order=2)\n7804 |         0     1.0\n7805 |         1     4.0\n7806 |         2     9.0\n7807 |         3    16.0\n7808 |         Name: d, dtype: float64\n7809 |         \"\"\"\n7810 |         inplace = validate_bool_kwarg(inplace, \"inplace\")\n7811 | \n7812 |         if inplace:\n7813 |             if not PYPY:\n7814 |                 if sys.getrefcount(self) <= REF_COUNT:\n7815 |                     warnings.warn(\n7816 |                         _chained_assignment_method_msg,\n7817 |                         ChainedAssignmentError,\n7818 |                         stacklevel=2,\n7819 |                     )\n7820 | \n7821 |         axis = self._get_axis_number(axis)\n7822 | \n7823 |         if self.empty:\n7824 |             if inplace:\n7825 |                 return None\n7826 |             return self.copy()\n7827 | \n7828 |         if not isinstance(method, str):\n7829 |             raise ValueError(\"'method' should be a string, not None.\")\n7830 | \n7831 |         obj, should_transpose = (self.T, True) if axis == 1 else (self, False)\n7832 | \n7833 |         if isinstance(obj.index, MultiIndex) and method != \"linear\":\n7834 |             raise ValueError(\n7835 |                 \"Only `method=linear` interpolation is supported on MultiIndexes.\"\n7836 |             )\n7837 | \n7838 |         limit_direction = missing.infer_limit_direction(limit_direction, method)\n7839 | \n7840 |         index = missing.get_interp_index(method, obj.index)\n7841 |         new_data = obj._mgr.interpolate(\n7842 |             method=method,\n7843 |             index=index,\n7844 |             limit=limit,\n7845 |             limit_direction=limit_direction,\n7846 |             limit_area=limit_area,\n7847 |             inplace=inplace,\n7848 |             **kwargs,\n7849 |         )\n7850 | \n7851 |         result = self._constructor_from_mgr(new_data, axes=new_data.axes)\n7852 |         if should_transpose:\n7853 |             result = result.T\n7854 |         if inplace:\n7855 |             return self._update_inplace(result)\n7856 |         else:\n7857 |             return result.__finalize__(self, method=\"interpolate\")\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me check the missing.py file since it contains key interpolation logic:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['pandas/core/missing.py']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"pandas/core/missing.py\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound file `pandas/core/missing.py`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `pandas/core/missing.py`.\n```\n   1 | \"\"\"\n   2 | Routines for filling missing data.\n   3 | \"\"\"\n   4 | \n   5 | from __future__ import annotations\n   6 | \n   7 | from functools import wraps\n   8 | from typing import (\n   9 |     TYPE_CHECKING,\n  10 |     Any,\n  11 |     Literal,\n  12 |     cast,\n  13 |     overload,\n  14 | )\n  15 | \n  16 | import numpy as np\n  17 | \n  18 | from pandas._libs import (\n  19 |     NaT,\n  20 |     algos,\n  21 |     lib,\n  22 | )\n  23 | from pandas._typing import (\n  24 |     ArrayLike,\n  25 |     AxisInt,\n  26 |     F,\n  27 |     ReindexMethod,\n  28 |     npt,\n  29 | )\n  30 | from pandas.compat._optional import import_optional_dependency\n  31 | \n  32 | from pandas.core.dtypes.cast import infer_dtype_from\n  33 | from pandas.core.dtypes.common import (\n  34 |     is_array_like,\n  35 |     is_bool_dtype,\n  36 |     is_numeric_dtype,\n  37 |     is_numeric_v_string_like,\n  38 |     is_object_dtype,\n  39 |     needs_i8_conversion,\n  40 | )\n  41 | from pandas.core.dtypes.dtypes import DatetimeTZDtype\n  42 | from pandas.core.dtypes.missing import (\n  43 |     is_valid_na_for_dtype,\n  44 |     isna,\n  45 |     na_value_for_dtype,\n  46 | )\n  47 | \n  48 | if TYPE_CHECKING:\n  49 |     from pandas import Index\n  50 | \n  51 | \n  52 | def check_value_size(value, mask: npt.NDArray[np.bool_], length: int):\n  53 |     \"\"\"\n  54 |     Validate the size of the values passed to ExtensionArray.fillna.\n  55 |     \"\"\"\n  56 |     if is_array_like(value):\n  57 |         if len(value) != length:\n  58 |             raise ValueError(\n  59 |                 f\"Length of 'value' does not match. Got ({len(value)}) \"\n  60 |                 f\" expected {length}\"\n  61 |             )\n  62 |         value = value[mask]\n  63 | \n  64 |     return value\n  65 | \n  66 | \n  67 | def mask_missing(arr: ArrayLike, values_to_mask) -> npt.NDArray[np.bool_]:\n  68 |     \"\"\"\n  69 |     Return a masking array of same size/shape as arr\n  70 |     with entries equaling any member of values_to_mask set to True\n  71 | \n  72 |     Parameters\n  73 |     ----------\n  74 |     arr : ArrayLike\n  75 |     values_to_mask: list, tuple, or scalar\n  76 | \n  77 |     Returns\n  78 |     -------\n  79 |     np.ndarray[bool]\n  80 |     \"\"\"\n  81 |     # When called from Block.replace/replace_list, values_to_mask is a scalar\n  82 |     #  known to be holdable by arr.\n  83 |     # When called from Series._single_replace, values_to_mask is tuple or list\n  84 |     dtype, values_to_mask = infer_dtype_from(values_to_mask)\n  85 | \n  86 |     if isinstance(dtype, np.dtype):\n  87 |         values_to_mask = np.array(values_to_mask, dtype=dtype)\n  88 |     else:\n  89 |         cls = dtype.construct_array_type()\n  90 |         if not lib.is_list_like(values_to_mask):\n  91 |             values_to_mask = [values_to_mask]\n  92 |         values_to_mask = cls._from_sequence(values_to_mask, dtype=dtype, copy=False)\n  93 | \n  94 |     potential_na = False\n  95 |     if is_object_dtype(arr.dtype):\n  96 |         # pre-compute mask to avoid comparison to NA\n  97 |         potential_na = True\n  98 |         arr_mask = ~isna(arr)\n  99 | \n 100 |     na_mask = isna(values_to_mask)\n 101 |     nonna = values_to_mask[~na_mask]\n 102 | \n 103 |     # GH 21977\n 104 |     mask = np.zeros(arr.shape, dtype=bool)\n 105 |     if (\n 106 |         is_numeric_dtype(arr.dtype)\n 107 |         and not is_bool_dtype(arr.dtype)\n 108 |         and is_bool_dtype(nonna.dtype)\n 109 |     ):\n 110 |         pass\n 111 |     elif (\n 112 |         is_bool_dtype(arr.dtype)\n 113 |         and is_numeric_dtype(nonna.dtype)\n 114 |         and not is_bool_dtype(nonna.dtype)\n 115 |     ):\n 116 |         pass\n 117 |     else:\n 118 |         for x in nonna:\n 119 |             if is_numeric_v_string_like(arr, x):\n 120 |                 # GH#29553 prevent numpy deprecation warnings\n 121 |                 pass\n 122 |             else:\n 123 |                 if potential_na:\n 124 |                     new_mask = np.zeros(arr.shape, dtype=np.bool_)\n 125 |                     new_mask[arr_mask] = arr[arr_mask] == x\n 126 |                 else:\n 127 |                     new_mask = arr == x\n 128 | \n 129 |                     if not isinstance(new_mask, np.ndarray):\n 130 |                         # usually BooleanArray\n 131 |                         new_mask = new_mask.to_numpy(dtype=bool, na_value=False)\n 132 |                 mask |= new_mask\n 133 | \n 134 |     if na_mask.any():\n 135 |         mask |= isna(arr)\n 136 | \n 137 |     return mask\n 138 | \n 139 | \n 140 | @overload\n 141 | def clean_fill_method(\n 142 |     method: Literal[\"ffill\", \"pad\", \"bfill\", \"backfill\"],\n 143 |     *,\n 144 |     allow_nearest: Literal[False] = ...,\n 145 | ) -> Literal[\"pad\", \"backfill\"]: ...\n 146 | \n 147 | \n 148 | @overload\n 149 | def clean_fill_method(\n 150 |     method: Literal[\"ffill\", \"pad\", \"bfill\", \"backfill\", \"nearest\"],\n 151 |     *,\n 152 |     allow_nearest: Literal[True],\n 153 | ) -> Literal[\"pad\", \"backfill\", \"nearest\"]: ...\n 154 | \n 155 | \n 156 | def clean_fill_method(\n 157 |     method: Literal[\"ffill\", \"pad\", \"bfill\", \"backfill\", \"nearest\"],\n 158 |     *,\n 159 |     allow_nearest: bool = False,\n 160 | ) -> Literal[\"pad\", \"backfill\", \"nearest\"]:\n 161 |     if isinstance(method, str):\n 162 |         # error: Incompatible types in assignment (expression has type \"str\", variable\n 163 |         # has type \"Literal['ffill', 'pad', 'bfill', 'backfill', 'nearest']\")\n 164 |         method = method.lower()  # type: ignore[assignment]\n 165 |         if method == \"ffill\":\n 166 |             method = \"pad\"\n 167 |         elif method == \"bfill\":\n 168 |             method = \"backfill\"\n 169 | \n 170 |     valid_methods = [\"pad\", \"backfill\"]\n 171 |     expecting = \"pad (ffill) or backfill (bfill)\"\n 172 |     if allow_nearest:\n 173 |         valid_methods.append(\"nearest\")\n 174 |         expecting = \"pad (ffill), backfill (bfill) or nearest\"\n 175 |     if method not in valid_methods:\n 176 |         raise ValueError(f\"Invalid fill method. Expecting {expecting}. Got {method}\")\n 177 |     return method\n 178 | \n 179 | \n 180 | # interpolation methods that dispatch to np.interp\n 181 | \n 182 | NP_METHODS = [\"linear\", \"time\", \"index\", \"values\"]\n 183 | \n 184 | # interpolation methods that dispatch to _interpolate_scipy_wrapper\n 185 | \n 186 | SP_METHODS = [\n 187 |     \"nearest\",\n 188 |     \"zero\",\n 189 |     \"slinear\",\n 190 |     \"quadratic\",\n 191 |     \"cubic\",\n 192 |     \"barycentric\",\n 193 |     \"krogh\",\n 194 |     \"spline\",\n 195 |     \"polynomial\",\n 196 |     \"from_derivatives\",\n 197 |     \"piecewise_polynomial\",\n 198 |     \"pchip\",\n 199 |     \"akima\",\n 200 |     \"cubicspline\",\n 201 | ]\n 202 | \n 203 | \n 204 | def clean_interp_method(method: str, index: Index, **kwargs) -> str:\n 205 |     order = kwargs.get(\"order\")\n 206 | \n 207 |     if method in (\"spline\", \"polynomial\") and order is None:\n 208 |         raise ValueError(\"You must specify the order of the spline or polynomial.\")\n 209 | \n 210 |     valid = NP_METHODS + SP_METHODS\n 211 |     if method not in valid:\n 212 |         raise ValueError(f\"method must be one of {valid}. Got '{method}' instead.\")\n 213 | \n 214 |     if method in (\"krogh\", \"piecewise_polynomial\", \"pchip\"):\n 215 |         if not index.is_monotonic_increasing:\n 216 |             raise ValueError(\n 217 |                 f\"{method} interpolation requires that the index be monotonic.\"\n 218 |             )\n 219 | \n 220 |     return method\n 221 | \n 222 | \n 223 | def find_valid_index(how: str, is_valid: npt.NDArray[np.bool_]) -> int | None:\n 224 |     \"\"\"\n 225 |     Retrieves the positional index of the first valid value.\n 226 | \n 227 |     Parameters\n 228 |     ----------\n 229 |     how : {'first', 'last'}\n 230 |         Use this parameter to change between the first or last valid index.\n 231 |     is_valid: np.ndarray\n 232 |         Mask to find na_values.\n 233 | \n 234 |     Returns\n 235 |     -------\n 236 |     int or None\n 237 |     \"\"\"\n 238 |     assert how in [\"first\", \"last\"]\n 239 | \n 240 |     if len(is_valid) == 0:  # early stop\n 241 |         return None\n 242 | \n 243 |     if is_valid.ndim == 2:\n 244 |         is_valid = is_valid.any(axis=1)  # reduce axis 1\n 245 | \n 246 |     if how == \"first\":\n 247 |         idxpos = is_valid[::].argmax()\n 248 | \n 249 |     elif how == \"last\":\n 250 |         idxpos = len(is_valid) - 1 - is_valid[::-1].argmax()\n 251 | \n 252 |     chk_notna = is_valid[idxpos]\n 253 | \n 254 |     if not chk_notna:\n 255 |         return None\n 256 |     # Incompatible return value type (got \"signedinteger[Any]\",\n 257 |     # expected \"Optional[int]\")\n 258 |     return idxpos  # type: ignore[return-value]\n 259 | \n 260 | \n 261 | def validate_limit_direction(\n 262 |     limit_direction: str,\n 263 | ) -> Literal[\"forward\", \"backward\", \"both\"]:\n 264 |     valid_limit_directions = [\"forward\", \"backward\", \"both\"]\n 265 |     limit_direction = limit_direction.lower()\n 266 |     if limit_direction not in valid_limit_directions:\n 267 |         raise ValueError(\n 268 |             \"Invalid limit_direction: expecting one of \"\n 269 |             f\"{valid_limit_directions}, got '{limit_direction}'.\"\n 270 |         )\n 271 |     # error: Incompatible return value type (got \"str\", expected\n 272 |     # \"Literal['forward', 'backward', 'both']\")\n 273 |     return limit_direction  # type: ignore[return-value]\n 274 | \n 275 | \n 276 | def validate_limit_area(limit_area: str | None) -> Literal[\"inside\", \"outside\"] | None:\n 277 |     if limit_area is not None:\n 278 |         valid_limit_areas = [\"inside\", \"outside\"]\n 279 |         limit_area = limit_area.lower()\n 280 |         if limit_area not in valid_limit_areas:\n 281 |             raise ValueError(\n 282 |                 f\"Invalid limit_area: expecting one of {valid_limit_areas}, got \"\n 283 |                 f\"{limit_area}.\"\n 284 |             )\n 285 |     # error: Incompatible return value type (got \"Optional[str]\", expected\n 286 |     # \"Optional[Literal['inside', 'outside']]\")\n 287 |     return limit_area  # type: ignore[return-value]\n 288 | \n 289 | \n 290 | def infer_limit_direction(\n 291 |     limit_direction: Literal[\"backward\", \"forward\", \"both\"] | None, method: str\n 292 | ) -> Literal[\"backward\", \"forward\", \"both\"]:\n 293 |     # Set `limit_direction` depending on `method`\n 294 |     if limit_direction is None:\n 295 |         if method in (\"backfill\", \"bfill\"):\n 296 |             limit_direction = \"backward\"\n 297 |         else:\n 298 |             limit_direction = \"forward\"\n 299 |     else:\n 300 |         if method in (\"pad\", \"ffill\") and limit_direction != \"forward\":\n 301 |             raise ValueError(\n 302 |                 f\"`limit_direction` must be 'forward' for method `{method}`\"\n 303 |             )\n 304 |         if method in (\"backfill\", \"bfill\") and limit_direction != \"backward\":\n 305 |             raise ValueError(\n 306 |                 f\"`limit_direction` must be 'backward' for method `{method}`\"\n 307 |             )\n 308 |     return limit_direction\n 309 | \n 310 | \n 311 | def get_interp_index(method, index: Index) -> Index:\n 312 |     # create/use the index\n 313 |     if method == \"linear\":\n 314 |         # prior default\n 315 |         from pandas import Index\n 316 | \n 317 |         if isinstance(index.dtype, DatetimeTZDtype) or lib.is_np_dtype(\n 318 |             index.dtype, \"mM\"\n 319 |         ):\n 320 |             # Convert datetime-like indexes to int64\n 321 |             index = Index(index.view(\"i8\"))\n 322 | \n 323 |         elif not is_numeric_dtype(index.dtype):\n 324 |             # We keep behavior consistent with prior versions of pandas for\n 325 |             # non-numeric, non-datetime indexes\n 326 |             index = Index(range(len(index)))\n 327 |     else:\n 328 |         methods = {\"index\", \"values\", \"nearest\", \"time\"}\n 329 |         is_numeric_or_datetime = (\n 330 |             is_numeric_dtype(index.dtype)\n 331 |             or isinstance(index.dtype, DatetimeTZDtype)\n 332 |             or lib.is_np_dtype(index.dtype, \"mM\")\n 333 |         )\n 334 |         valid = NP_METHODS + SP_METHODS\n 335 |         if method in valid:\n 336 |             if method not in methods and not is_numeric_or_datetime:\n 337 |                 raise ValueError(\n 338 |                     \"Index column must be numeric or datetime type when \"\n 339 |                     f\"using {method} method other than linear. \"\n 340 |                     \"Try setting a numeric or datetime index column before \"\n 341 |                     \"interpolating.\"\n 342 |                 )\n 343 |         else:\n 344 |             raise ValueError(f\"Can not interpolate with method={method}.\")\n 345 | \n 346 |     if isna(index).any():\n 347 |         raise NotImplementedError(\n 348 |             \"Interpolation with NaNs in the index \"\n 349 |             \"has not been implemented. Try filling \"\n 350 |             \"those NaNs before interpolating.\"\n 351 |         )\n 352 |     return index\n 353 | \n 354 | \n 355 | def interpolate_2d_inplace(\n 356 |     data: np.ndarray,  # floating dtype\n 357 |     index: Index,\n 358 |     axis: AxisInt,\n 359 |     method: str = \"linear\",\n 360 |     limit: int | None = None,\n 361 |     limit_direction: str = \"forward\",\n 362 |     limit_area: str | None = None,\n 363 |     fill_value: Any | None = None,\n 364 |     mask=None,\n 365 |     **kwargs,\n 366 | ) -> None:\n 367 |     \"\"\"\n 368 |     Column-wise application of _interpolate_1d.\n 369 | \n 370 |     Notes\n 371 |     -----\n 372 |     Alters 'data' in-place.\n 373 | \n 374 |     The signature does differ from _interpolate_1d because it only\n 375 |     includes what is needed for Block.interpolate.\n 376 |     \"\"\"\n 377 |     # validate the interp method\n 378 |     clean_interp_method(method, index, **kwargs)\n 379 | \n 380 |     if is_valid_na_for_dtype(fill_value, data.dtype):\n 381 |         fill_value = na_value_for_dtype(data.dtype, compat=False)\n 382 | \n 383 |     if method == \"time\":\n 384 |         if not needs_i8_conversion(index.dtype):\n 385 |             raise ValueError(\n 386 |                 \"time-weighted interpolation only works \"\n 387 |                 \"on Series or DataFrames with a \"\n 388 |                 \"DatetimeIndex\"\n 389 |             )\n 390 |         method = \"values\"\n 391 | \n 392 |     limit_direction = validate_limit_direction(limit_direction)\n 393 |     limit_area_validated = validate_limit_area(limit_area)\n 394 | \n 395 |     # default limit is unlimited GH #16282\n 396 |     limit = algos.validate_limit(nobs=None, limit=limit)\n 397 | \n 398 |     indices = _index_to_interp_indices(index, method)\n 399 | \n 400 |     def func(yvalues: np.ndarray) -> None:\n 401 |         # process 1-d slices in the axis direction\n 402 | \n 403 |         _interpolate_1d(\n 404 |             indices=indices,\n 405 |             yvalues=yvalues,\n 406 |             method=method,\n 407 |             limit=limit,\n 408 |             limit_direction=limit_direction,\n 409 |             limit_area=limit_area_validated,\n 410 |             fill_value=fill_value,\n 411 |             bounds_error=False,\n 412 |             mask=mask,\n 413 |             **kwargs,\n 414 |         )\n 415 | \n 416 |     # error: No overload variant of \"apply_along_axis\" matches\n 417 |     # argument types \"Callable[[ndarray[Any, Any]], None]\",\n 418 |     # \"int\", \"ndarray[Any, Any]\"\n 419 |     np.apply_along_axis(func, axis, data)  # type: ignore[call-overload]\n 420 | \n 421 | \n 422 | def _index_to_interp_indices(index: Index, method: str) -> np.ndarray:\n 423 |     \"\"\"\n 424 |     Convert Index to ndarray of indices to pass to NumPy/SciPy.\n 425 |     \"\"\"\n 426 |     xarr = index._values\n 427 |     if needs_i8_conversion(xarr.dtype):\n 428 |         # GH#1646 for dt64tz\n 429 |         xarr = xarr.view(\"i8\")\n 430 | \n 431 |     if method == \"linear\":\n 432 |         inds = xarr\n 433 |         inds = cast(np.ndarray, inds)\n 434 |     else:\n 435 |         inds = np.asarray(xarr)\n 436 | \n 437 |         if method in (\"values\", \"index\"):\n 438 |             if inds.dtype == np.object_:\n 439 |                 inds = lib.maybe_convert_objects(inds)\n 440 | \n 441 |     return inds\n 442 | \n 443 | \n 444 | def _interpolate_1d(\n 445 |     indices: np.ndarray,\n 446 |     yvalues: np.ndarray,\n 447 |     method: str = \"linear\",\n 448 |     limit: int | None = None,\n 449 |     limit_direction: str = \"forward\",\n 450 |     limit_area: Literal[\"inside\", \"outside\"] | None = None,\n 451 |     fill_value: Any | None = None,\n 452 |     bounds_error: bool = False,\n 453 |     order: int | None = None,\n 454 |     mask=None,\n 455 |     **kwargs,\n 456 | ) -> None:\n 457 |     \"\"\"\n 458 |     Logic for the 1-d interpolation.  The input\n 459 |     indices and yvalues will each be 1-d arrays of the same length.\n 460 | \n 461 |     Bounds_error is currently hardcoded to False since non-scipy ones don't\n 462 |     take it as an argument.\n 463 | \n 464 |     Notes\n 465 |     -----\n 466 |     Fills 'yvalues' in-place.\n 467 |     \"\"\"\n 468 |     if mask is not None:\n 469 |         invalid = mask\n 470 |     else:\n 471 |         invalid = isna(yvalues)\n 472 |     valid = ~invalid\n 473 | \n 474 |     if not valid.any():\n 475 |         return\n 476 | \n 477 |     if valid.all():\n 478 |         return\n 479 | \n 480 |     # These index pointers to invalid values... i.e. {0, 1, etc...\n 481 |     all_nans = np.flatnonzero(invalid)\n 482 | \n 483 |     first_valid_index = find_valid_index(how=\"first\", is_valid=valid)\n 484 |     if first_valid_index is None:  # no nan found in start\n 485 |         first_valid_index = 0\n 486 |     start_nans = np.arange(first_valid_index)\n 487 | \n 488 |     last_valid_index = find_valid_index(how=\"last\", is_valid=valid)\n 489 |     if last_valid_index is None:  # no nan found in end\n 490 |         last_valid_index = len(yvalues)\n 491 |     end_nans = np.arange(1 + last_valid_index, len(valid))\n 492 | \n 493 |     # preserve_nans contains indices of invalid values,\n 494 |     # but in this case, it is the final set of indices that need to be\n 495 |     # preserved as NaN after the interpolation.\n 496 | \n 497 |     # For example if limit_direction='forward' then preserve_nans will\n 498 |     # contain indices of NaNs at the beginning of the series, and NaNs that\n 499 |     # are more than 'limit' away from the prior non-NaN.\n 500 | \n 501 |     # set preserve_nans based on direction using _interp_limit\n 502 |     if limit_direction == \"forward\":\n 503 |         preserve_nans = np.union1d(start_nans, _interp_limit(invalid, limit, 0))\n 504 |     elif limit_direction == \"backward\":\n 505 |         preserve_nans = np.union1d(end_nans, _interp_limit(invalid, 0, limit))\n 506 |     else:\n 507 |         # both directions... just use _interp_limit\n 508 |         preserve_nans = np.unique(_interp_limit(invalid, limit, limit))\n 509 | \n 510 |     # if limit_area is set, add either mid or outside indices\n 511 |     # to preserve_nans GH #16284\n 512 |     if limit_area == \"inside\":\n 513 |         # preserve NaNs on the outside\n 514 |         preserve_nans = np.union1d(preserve_nans, start_nans)\n 515 |         preserve_nans = np.union1d(preserve_nans, end_nans)\n 516 |     elif limit_area == \"outside\":\n 517 |         # preserve NaNs on the inside\n 518 |         mid_nans = np.setdiff1d(all_nans, start_nans, assume_unique=True)\n 519 |         mid_nans = np.setdiff1d(mid_nans, end_nans, assume_unique=True)\n 520 |         preserve_nans = np.union1d(preserve_nans, mid_nans)\n 521 | \n 522 |     is_datetimelike = yvalues.dtype.kind in \"mM\"\n 523 | \n 524 |     if is_datetimelike:\n 525 |         yvalues = yvalues.view(\"i8\")\n 526 | \n 527 |     if method in NP_METHODS:\n 528 |         # np.interp requires sorted X values, #21037\n 529 | \n 530 |         indexer = np.argsort(indices[valid])\n 531 |         yvalues[invalid] = np.interp(\n 532 |             indices[invalid], indices[valid][indexer], yvalues[valid][indexer]\n 533 |         )\n 534 |     else:\n 535 |         yvalues[invalid] = _interpolate_scipy_wrapper(\n 536 |             indices[valid],\n 537 |             yvalues[valid],\n 538 |             indices[invalid],\n 539 |             method=method,\n 540 |             fill_value=fill_value,\n 541 |             bounds_error=bounds_error,\n 542 |             order=order,\n 543 |             **kwargs,\n 544 |         )\n 545 | \n 546 |     if mask is not None:\n 547 |         mask[:] = False\n 548 |         mask[preserve_nans] = True\n 549 |     elif is_datetimelike:\n 550 |         yvalues[preserve_nans] = NaT.value\n 551 |     else:\n 552 |         yvalues[preserve_nans] = np.nan\n 553 |     return\n 554 | \n 555 | \n 556 | def _interpolate_scipy_wrapper(\n 557 |     x: np.ndarray,\n 558 |     y: np.ndarray,\n 559 |     new_x: np.ndarray,\n 560 |     method: str,\n 561 |     fill_value=None,\n 562 |     bounds_error: bool = False,\n 563 |     order=None,\n 564 |     **kwargs,\n 565 | ):\n 566 |     \"\"\"\n 567 |     Passed off to scipy.interpolate.interp1d. method is scipy's kind.\n 568 |     Returns an array interpolated at new_x.  Add any new methods to\n 569 |     the list in _clean_interp_method.\n 570 |     \"\"\"\n 571 |     extra = f\"{method} interpolation requires SciPy.\"\n 572 |     import_optional_dependency(\"scipy\", extra=extra)\n 573 |     from scipy import interpolate\n 574 | \n 575 |     new_x = np.asarray(new_x)\n 576 | \n 577 |     # ignores some kwargs that could be passed along.\n 578 |     alt_methods = {\n 579 |         \"barycentric\": interpolate.barycentric_interpolate,\n 580 |         \"krogh\": interpolate.krogh_interpolate,\n 581 |         \"from_derivatives\": _from_derivatives,\n 582 |         \"piecewise_polynomial\": _from_derivatives,\n 583 |         \"cubicspline\": _cubicspline_interpolate,\n 584 |         \"akima\": _akima_interpolate,\n 585 |         \"pchip\": interpolate.pchip_interpolate,\n 586 |     }\n 587 | \n 588 |     interp1d_methods = [\n 589 |         \"nearest\",\n 590 |         \"zero\",\n 591 |         \"slinear\",\n 592 |         \"quadratic\",\n 593 |         \"cubic\",\n 594 |         \"polynomial\",\n 595 |     ]\n 596 |     if method in interp1d_methods:\n 597 |         if method == \"polynomial\":\n 598 |             kind = order\n 599 |         else:\n 600 |             kind = method\n 601 |         terp = interpolate.interp1d(\n 602 |             x, y, kind=kind, fill_value=fill_value, bounds_error=bounds_error\n 603 |         )\n 604 |         new_y = terp(new_x)\n 605 |     elif method == \"spline\":\n 606 |         # GH #10633, #24014\n 607 |         if isna(order) or (order <= 0):\n 608 |             raise ValueError(\n 609 |                 f\"order needs to be specified and greater than 0; got order: {order}\"\n 610 |             )\n 611 |         terp = interpolate.UnivariateSpline(x, y, k=order, **kwargs)\n 612 |         new_y = terp(new_x)\n 613 |     else:\n 614 |         # GH 7295: need to be able to write for some reason\n 615 |         # in some circumstances: check all three\n 616 |         if not x.flags.writeable:\n 617 |             x = x.copy()\n 618 |         if not y.flags.writeable:\n 619 |             y = y.copy()\n 620 |         if not new_x.flags.writeable:\n 621 |             new_x = new_x.copy()\n 622 |         terp = alt_methods.get(method, None)\n 623 |         if terp is None:\n 624 |             raise ValueError(f\"Can not interpolate with method={method}.\")\n 625 | \n 626 |         # Make sure downcast is not in kwargs for alt methods\n 627 |         kwargs.pop(\"downcast\", None)\n 628 |         new_y = terp(x, y, new_x, **kwargs)\n 629 |     return new_y\n 630 | \n 631 | \n 632 | def _from_derivatives(\n 633 |     xi: np.ndarray,\n 634 |     yi: np.ndarray,\n 635 |     x: np.ndarray,\n 636 |     order=None,\n 637 |     der: int | list[int] | None = 0,\n 638 |     extrapolate: bool = False,\n 639 | ):\n 640 |     \"\"\"\n 641 |     Convenience function for interpolate.BPoly.from_derivatives.\n 642 | \n 643 |     Construct a piecewise polynomial in the Bernstein basis, compatible\n 644 |     with the specified values and derivatives at breakpoints.\n 645 | \n 646 |     Parameters\n 647 |     ----------\n 648 |     xi : array-like\n 649 |         sorted 1D array of x-coordinates\n 650 |     yi : array-like or list of array-likes\n 651 |         yi[i][j] is the j-th derivative known at xi[i]\n 652 |     order: None or int or array-like of ints. Default: None.\n 653 |         Specifies the degree of local polynomials. If not None, some\n 654 |         derivatives are ignored.\n 655 |     der : int or list\n 656 |         How many derivatives to extract; None for all potentially nonzero\n 657 |         derivatives (that is a number equal to the number of points), or a\n 658 |         list of derivatives to extract. This number includes the function\n 659 |         value as 0th derivative.\n 660 |      extrapolate : bool, optional\n 661 |         Whether to extrapolate to ouf-of-bounds points based on first and last\n 662 |         intervals, or to return NaNs. Default: True.\n 663 | \n 664 |     See Also\n 665 |     --------\n 666 |     scipy.interpolate.BPoly.from_derivatives\n 667 | \n 668 |     Returns\n 669 |     -------\n 670 |     y : scalar or array-like\n 671 |         The result, of length R or length M or M by R.\n 672 |     \"\"\"\n 673 |     from scipy import interpolate\n 674 | \n 675 |     # return the method for compat with scipy version & backwards compat\n 676 |     method = interpolate.BPoly.from_derivatives\n 677 |     m = method(xi, yi.reshape(-1, 1), orders=order, extrapolate=extrapolate)\n 678 | \n 679 |     return m(x)\n 680 | \n 681 | \n 682 | def _akima_interpolate(\n 683 |     xi: np.ndarray,\n 684 |     yi: np.ndarray,\n 685 |     x: np.ndarray,\n 686 |     der: int | list[int] | None = 0,\n 687 |     axis: AxisInt = 0,\n 688 | ):\n 689 |     \"\"\"\n 690 |     Convenience function for akima interpolation.\n 691 |     xi and yi are arrays of values used to approximate some function f,\n 692 |     with ``yi = f(xi)``.\n 693 | \n 694 |     See `Akima1DInterpolator` for details.\n 695 | \n 696 |     Parameters\n 697 |     ----------\n 698 |     xi : np.ndarray\n 699 |         A sorted list of x-coordinates, of length N.\n 700 |     yi : np.ndarray\n 701 |         A 1-D array of real values.  `yi`'s length along the interpolation\n 702 |         axis must be equal to the length of `xi`. If N-D array, use axis\n 703 |         parameter to select correct axis.\n 704 |     x : np.ndarray\n 705 |         Of length M.\n 706 |     der : int, optional\n 707 |         How many derivatives to extract; None for all potentially\n 708 |         nonzero derivatives (that is a number equal to the number\n 709 |         of points), or a list of derivatives to extract. This number\n 710 |         includes the function value as 0th derivative.\n 711 |     axis : int, optional\n 712 |         Axis in the yi array corresponding to the x-coordinate values.\n 713 | \n 714 |     See Also\n 715 |     --------\n 716 |     scipy.interpolate.Akima1DInterpolator\n 717 | \n 718 |     Returns\n 719 |     -------\n 720 |     y : scalar or array-like\n 721 |         The result, of length R or length M or M by R,\n 722 | \n 723 |     \"\"\"\n 724 |     from scipy import interpolate\n 725 | \n 726 |     P = interpolate.Akima1DInterpolator(xi, yi, axis=axis)\n 727 | \n 728 |     return P(x, nu=der)\n 729 | \n 730 | \n 731 | def _cubicspline_interpolate(\n 732 |     xi: np.ndarray,\n 733 |     yi: np.ndarray,\n 734 |     x: np.ndarray,\n 735 |     axis: AxisInt = 0,\n 736 |     bc_type: str | tuple[Any, Any] = \"not-a-knot\",\n 737 |     extrapolate=None,\n 738 | ):\n 739 |     \"\"\"\n 740 |     Convenience function for cubic spline data interpolator.\n 741 | \n 742 |     See `scipy.interpolate.CubicSpline` for details.\n 743 | \n 744 |     Parameters\n 745 |     ----------\n 746 |     xi : np.ndarray, shape (n,)\n 747 |         1-d array containing values of the independent variable.\n 748 |         Values must be real, finite and in strictly increasing order.\n 749 |     yi : np.ndarray\n 750 |         Array containing values of the dependent variable. It can have\n 751 |         arbitrary number of dimensions, but the length along ``axis``\n 752 |         (see below) must match the length of ``x``. Values must be finite.\n 753 |     x : np.ndarray, shape (m,)\n 754 |     axis : int, optional\n 755 |         Axis along which `y` is assumed to be varying. Meaning that for\n 756 |         ``x[i]`` the corresponding values are ``np.take(y, i, axis=axis)``.\n 757 |         Default is 0.\n 758 |     bc_type : string or 2-tuple, optional\n 759 |         Boundary condition type. Two additional equations, given by the\n 760 |         boundary conditions, are required to determine all coefficients of\n 761 |         polynomials on each segment [2]_.\n 762 |         If `bc_type` is a string, then the specified condition will be applied\n 763 |         at both ends of a spline. Available conditions are:\n 764 |         * 'not-a-knot' (default): The first and second segment at a curve end\n 765 |           are the same polynomial. It is a good default when there is no\n 766 |           information on boundary conditions.\n 767 |         * 'periodic': The interpolated functions is assumed to be periodic\n 768 |           of period ``x[-1] - x[0]``. The first and last value of `y` must be\n 769 |           identical: ``y[0] == y[-1]``. This boundary condition will result in\n 770 |           ``y'[0] == y'[-1]`` and ``y''[0] == y''[-1]``.\n 771 |         * 'clamped': The first derivative at curves ends are zero. Assuming\n 772 |           a 1D `y`, ``bc_type=((1, 0.0), (1, 0.0))`` is the same condition.\n 773 |         * 'natural': The second derivative at curve ends are zero. Assuming\n 774 |           a 1D `y`, ``bc_type=((2, 0.0), (2, 0.0))`` is the same condition.\n 775 |         If `bc_type` is a 2-tuple, the first and the second value will be\n 776 |         applied at the curve start and end respectively. The tuple values can\n 777 |         be one of the previously mentioned strings (except 'periodic') or a\n 778 |         tuple `(order, deriv_values)` allowing to specify arbitrary\n 779 |         derivatives at curve ends:\n 780 |         * `order`: the derivative order, 1 or 2.\n 781 |         * `deriv_value`: array-like containing derivative values, shape must\n 782 |           be the same as `y`, excluding ``axis`` dimension. For example, if\n 783 |           `y` is 1D, then `deriv_value` must be a scalar. If `y` is 3D with\n 784 |           the shape (n0, n1, n2) and axis=2, then `deriv_value` must be 2D\n 785 |           and have the shape (n0, n1).\n 786 |     extrapolate : {bool, 'periodic', None}, optional\n 787 |         If bool, determines whether to extrapolate to out-of-bounds points\n 788 |         based on first and last intervals, or to return NaNs. If 'periodic',\n 789 |         periodic extrapolation is used. If None (default), ``extrapolate`` is\n 790 |         set to 'periodic' for ``bc_type='periodic'`` and to True otherwise.\n 791 | \n 792 |     See Also\n 793 |     --------\n 794 |     scipy.interpolate.CubicHermiteSpline\n 795 | \n 796 |     Returns\n 797 |     -------\n 798 |     y : scalar or array-like\n 799 |         The result, of shape (m,)\n 800 | \n 801 |     References\n 802 |     ----------\n 803 |     .. [1] `Cubic Spline Interpolation\n 804 |             <https://en.wikiversity.org/wiki/Cubic_Spline_Interpolation>`_\n 805 |             on Wikiversity.\n 806 |     .. [2] Carl de Boor, \"A Practical Guide to Splines\", Springer-Verlag, 1978.\n 807 |     \"\"\"\n 808 |     from scipy import interpolate\n 809 | \n 810 |     P = interpolate.CubicSpline(\n 811 |         xi, yi, axis=axis, bc_type=bc_type, extrapolate=extrapolate\n 812 |     )\n 813 | \n 814 |     return P(x)\n 815 | \n 816 | \n 817 | def pad_or_backfill_inplace(\n 818 |     values: np.ndarray,\n 819 |     method: Literal[\"pad\", \"backfill\"] = \"pad\",\n 820 |     axis: AxisInt = 0,\n 821 |     limit: int | None = None,\n 822 |     limit_area: Literal[\"inside\", \"outside\"] | None = None,\n 823 | ) -> None:\n 824 |     \"\"\"\n 825 |     Perform an actual interpolation of values, values will be make 2-d if\n 826 |     needed fills inplace, returns the result.\n 827 | \n 828 |     Parameters\n 829 |     ----------\n 830 |     values: np.ndarray\n 831 |         Input array.\n 832 |     method: str, default \"pad\"\n 833 |         Interpolation method. Could be \"bfill\" or \"pad\"\n 834 |     axis: 0 or 1\n 835 |         Interpolation axis\n 836 |     limit: int, optional\n 837 |         Index limit on interpolation.\n 838 |     limit_area: str, optional\n 839 |         Limit area for interpolation. Can be \"inside\" or \"outside\"\n 840 | \n 841 |     Notes\n 842 |     -----\n 843 |     Modifies values in-place.\n 844 |     \"\"\"\n 845 |     transf = (lambda x: x) if axis == 0 else (lambda x: x.T)\n 846 | \n 847 |     # reshape a 1 dim if needed\n 848 |     if values.ndim == 1:\n 849 |         if axis != 0:  # pragma: no cover\n 850 |             raise AssertionError(\"cannot interpolate on a ndim == 1 with axis != 0\")\n 851 |         values = values.reshape(tuple((1,) + values.shape))\n 852 | \n 853 |     method = clean_fill_method(method)\n 854 |     tvalues = transf(values)\n 855 | \n 856 |     func = get_fill_func(method, ndim=2)\n 857 |     # _pad_2d and _backfill_2d both modify tvalues inplace\n 858 |     func(tvalues, limit=limit, limit_area=limit_area)\n 859 | \n 860 | \n 861 | def _fillna_prep(\n 862 |     values, mask: npt.NDArray[np.bool_] | None = None\n 863 | ) -> npt.NDArray[np.bool_]:\n 864 |     # boilerplate for _pad_1d, _backfill_1d, _pad_2d, _backfill_2d\n 865 | \n 866 |     if mask is None:\n 867 |         mask = isna(values)\n 868 | \n 869 |     return mask\n 870 | \n 871 | \n 872 | def _datetimelike_compat(func: F) -> F:\n 873 |     \"\"\"\n 874 |     Wrapper to handle datetime64 and timedelta64 dtypes.\n 875 |     \"\"\"\n 876 | \n 877 |     @wraps(func)\n 878 |     def new_func(\n 879 |         values,\n 880 |         limit: int | None = None,\n 881 |         limit_area: Literal[\"inside\", \"outside\"] | None = None,\n 882 |         mask=None,\n 883 |     ):\n 884 |         if needs_i8_conversion(values.dtype):\n 885 |             if mask is None:\n 886 |                 # This needs to occur before casting to int64\n 887 |                 mask = isna(values)\n 888 | \n 889 |             result, mask = func(\n 890 |                 values.view(\"i8\"), limit=limit, limit_area=limit_area, mask=mask\n 891 |             )\n 892 |             return result.view(values.dtype), mask\n 893 | \n 894 |         return func(values, limit=limit, limit_area=limit_area, mask=mask)\n 895 | \n 896 |     return cast(F, new_func)\n 897 | \n 898 | \n 899 | @_datetimelike_compat\n 900 | def _pad_1d(\n 901 |     values: np.ndarray,\n 902 |     limit: int | None = None,\n 903 |     limit_area: Literal[\"inside\", \"outside\"] | None = None,\n 904 |     mask: npt.NDArray[np.bool_] | None = None,\n 905 | ) -> tuple[np.ndarray, npt.NDArray[np.bool_]]:\n 906 |     mask = _fillna_prep(values, mask)\n 907 |     if limit_area is not None and not mask.all():\n 908 |         _fill_limit_area_1d(mask, limit_area)\n 909 |     algos.pad_inplace(values, mask, limit=limit)\n 910 |     return values, mask\n 911 | \n 912 | \n 913 | @_datetimelike_compat\n 914 | def _backfill_1d(\n 915 |     values: np.ndarray,\n 916 |     limit: int | None = None,\n 917 |     limit_area: Literal[\"inside\", \"outside\"] | None = None,\n 918 |     mask: npt.NDArray[np.bool_] | None = None,\n 919 | ) -> tuple[np.ndarray, npt.NDArray[np.bool_]]:\n 920 |     mask = _fillna_prep(values, mask)\n 921 |     if limit_area is not None and not mask.all():\n 922 |         _fill_limit_area_1d(mask, limit_area)\n 923 |     algos.backfill_inplace(values, mask, limit=limit)\n 924 |     return values, mask\n 925 | \n 926 | \n 927 | @_datetimelike_compat\n 928 | def _pad_2d(\n 929 |     values: np.ndarray,\n 930 |     limit: int | None = None,\n 931 |     limit_area: Literal[\"inside\", \"outside\"] | None = None,\n 932 |     mask: npt.NDArray[np.bool_] | None = None,\n 933 | ) -> tuple[np.ndarray, npt.NDArray[np.bool_]]:\n 934 |     mask = _fillna_prep(values, mask)\n 935 |     if limit_area is not None:\n 936 |         _fill_limit_area_2d(mask, limit_area)\n 937 | \n 938 |     if values.size:\n 939 |         algos.pad_2d_inplace(values, mask, limit=limit)\n 940 |     return values, mask\n 941 | \n 942 | \n 943 | @_datetimelike_compat\n 944 | def _backfill_2d(\n 945 |     values,\n 946 |     limit: int | None = None,\n 947 |     limit_area: Literal[\"inside\", \"outside\"] | None = None,\n 948 |     mask: npt.NDArray[np.bool_] | None = None,\n 949 | ):\n 950 |     mask = _fillna_prep(values, mask)\n 951 |     if limit_area is not None:\n 952 |         _fill_limit_area_2d(mask, limit_area)\n 953 | \n 954 |     if values.size:\n 955 |         algos.backfill_2d_inplace(values, mask, limit=limit)\n 956 |     else:\n 957 |         # for test coverage\n 958 |         pass\n 959 |     return values, mask\n 960 | \n 961 | \n 962 | def _fill_limit_area_1d(\n 963 |     mask: npt.NDArray[np.bool_], limit_area: Literal[\"outside\", \"inside\"]\n 964 | ) -> None:\n 965 |     \"\"\"Prepare 1d mask for ffill/bfill with limit_area.\n 966 | \n 967 |     Caller is responsible for checking at least one value of mask is False.\n 968 |     When called, mask will no longer faithfully represent when\n 969 |     the corresponding are NA or not.\n 970 | \n 971 |     Parameters\n 972 |     ----------\n 973 |     mask : np.ndarray[bool, ndim=1]\n 974 |         Mask representing NA values when filling.\n 975 |     limit_area : { \"outside\", \"inside\" }\n 976 |         Whether to limit filling to outside or inside the outer most non-NA value.\n 977 |     \"\"\"\n 978 |     neg_mask = ~mask\n 979 |     first = neg_mask.argmax()\n 980 |     last = len(neg_mask) - neg_mask[::-1].argmax() - 1\n 981 |     if limit_area == \"inside\":\n 982 |         mask[:first] = False\n 983 |         mask[last + 1 :] = False\n 984 |     elif limit_area == \"outside\":\n 985 |         mask[first + 1 : last] = False\n 986 | \n 987 | \n 988 | def _fill_limit_area_2d(\n 989 |     mask: npt.NDArray[np.bool_], limit_area: Literal[\"outside\", \"inside\"]\n 990 | ) -> None:\n 991 |     \"\"\"Prepare 2d mask for ffill/bfill with limit_area.\n 992 | \n 993 |     When called, mask will no longer faithfully represent when\n 994 |     the corresponding are NA or not.\n 995 | \n 996 |     Parameters\n 997 |     ----------\n 998 |     mask : np.ndarray[bool, ndim=1]\n 999 |         Mask representing NA values when filling.\n1000 |     limit_area : { \"outside\", \"inside\" }\n1001 |         Whether to limit filling to outside or inside the outer most non-NA value.\n1002 |     \"\"\"\n1003 |     neg_mask = ~mask.T\n1004 |     if limit_area == \"outside\":\n1005 |         # Identify inside\n1006 |         la_mask = (\n1007 |             np.maximum.accumulate(neg_mask, axis=0)\n1008 |             & np.maximum.accumulate(neg_mask[::-1], axis=0)[::-1]\n1009 |         )\n1010 |     else:\n1011 |         # Identify outside\n1012 |         la_mask = (\n1013 |             ~np.maximum.accumulate(neg_mask, axis=0)\n1014 |             | ~np.maximum.accumulate(neg_mask[::-1], axis=0)[::-1]\n1015 |         )\n1016 |     mask[la_mask.T] = False\n1017 | \n1018 | \n1019 | _fill_methods = {\"pad\": _pad_1d, \"backfill\": _backfill_1d}\n1020 | \n1021 | \n1022 | def get_fill_func(method, ndim: int = 1):\n1023 |     method = clean_fill_method(method)\n1024 |     if ndim == 1:\n1025 |         return _fill_methods[method]\n1026 |     return {\"pad\": _pad_2d, \"backfill\": _backfill_2d}[method]\n1027 | \n1028 | \n1029 | def clean_reindex_fill_method(method) -> ReindexMethod | None:\n1030 |     if method is None:\n1031 |         return None\n1032 |     return clean_fill_method(method, allow_nearest=True)\n1033 | \n1034 | \n1035 | def _interp_limit(\n1036 |     invalid: npt.NDArray[np.bool_], fw_limit: int | None, bw_limit: int | None\n1037 | ) -> np.ndarray:\n1038 |     \"\"\"\n1039 |     Get indexers of values that won't be filled\n1040 |     because they exceed the limits.\n1041 | \n1042 |     Parameters\n1043 |     ----------\n1044 |     invalid : np.ndarray[bool]\n1045 |     fw_limit : int or None\n1046 |         forward limit to index\n1047 |     bw_limit : int or None\n1048 |         backward limit to index\n1049 | \n1050 |     Returns\n1051 |     -------\n1052 |     set of indexers\n1053 | \n1054 |     Notes\n1055 |     -----\n1056 |     This is equivalent to the more readable, but slower\n1057 | \n1058 |     .. code-block:: python\n1059 | \n1060 |         def _interp_limit(invalid, fw_limit, bw_limit):\n1061 |             for x in np.where(invalid)[0]:\n1062 |                 if invalid[max(0, x - fw_limit) : x + bw_limit + 1].all():\n1063 |                     yield x\n1064 |     \"\"\"\n1065 |     # handle forward first; the backward direction is the same except\n1066 |     # 1. operate on the reversed array\n1067 |     # 2. subtract the returned indices from N - 1\n1068 |     N = len(invalid)\n1069 |     f_idx = np.array([], dtype=np.int64)\n1070 |     b_idx = np.array([], dtype=np.int64)\n1071 |     assume_unique = True\n1072 | \n1073 |     def inner(invalid, limit: int):\n1074 |         limit = min(limit, N)\n1075 |         windowed = np.lib.stride_tricks.sliding_window_view(invalid, limit + 1).all(1)\n1076 |         idx = np.union1d(\n1077 |             np.where(windowed)[0] + limit,\n1078 |             np.where((~invalid[: limit + 1]).cumsum() == 0)[0],\n1079 |         )\n1080 |         return idx\n1081 | \n1082 |     if fw_limit is not None:\n1083 |         if fw_limit == 0:\n1084 |             f_idx = np.where(invalid)[0]\n1085 |             assume_unique = False\n1086 |         else:\n1087 |             f_idx = inner(invalid, fw_limit)\n1088 | \n1089 |     if bw_limit is not None:\n1090 |         if bw_limit == 0:\n1091 |             # then we don't even need to care about backwards\n1092 |             # just use forwards\n1093 |             return f_idx\n1094 |         else:\n1095 |             b_idx = N - 1 - inner(invalid[::-1], bw_limit)\n1096 |             if fw_limit == 0:\n1097 |                 return b_idx\n1098 | \n1099 |     return np.intersect1d(f_idx, b_idx, assume_unique=assume_unique)\n1100 | \n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me analyze the key components that need documentation improvement:\n\n1. Main interpolation method in `pandas/core/generic.py:NDFrame.interpolate`\n2. Core interpolation implementation in `pandas/core/missing.py`\n3. Time-based interpolation handling in `get_interp_index` and `interpolate_2d_inplace`\n4. Method validation and cleanup in `clean_interp_method`\n5. Special case handling for different index types and methods\n\nBased on the above analysis, here are the specific locations that need documentation improvement, ordered by importance:\n\n```\npandas/core/generic.py\nline: 7646-7861\nfunction: NDFrame.interpolate\n\npandas/core/missing.py\nline: 311-352\nfunction: get_interp_index\n\npandas/core/missing.py\nline: 355-421\nfunction: interpolate_2d_inplace\n\npandas/core/missing.py\nline: 204-220\nfunction: clean_interp_method\n\npandas/core/missing.py\nline: 182-186\nvariable: NP_METHODS, SP_METHODS\n```\n\nThe issue focuses on improving the documentation for several aspects:\n1. The main `interpolate()` method in `generic.py` needs clearer explanation of its overall functionality and parameters\n2. The special methods ('time', 'index', 'values') and how they work need better documentation\n3. The implementation details of time-based interpolation need clarification\n4. The method validation and cleanup functions need better explanations\n5. The constant definitions for supported methods need clarification\n\nThese locations cover both the high-level documentation and the technical implementation details that users would need to understand when working with the interpolation functionality.\n\n<function=finish>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "explore_tree_structure", "description": "\nUnified repository exploring tool that traverses a pre-built code graph to retrieve dependency structure around specified entities.\nThe search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n\nCode Graph Definition:\n* Entity Types: 'directory', 'file', 'class', 'function'.\n* Dependency Types: 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits'.\n* Hierarchy:\n    - Directories contain files and subdirectories.\n    - Files contain classes and functions.\n    - Classes contain inner classes and methods.\n    - Functions can contain inner functions.\n* Interactions:\n    - Files/classes/functions can import classes and functions.\n    - Classes can inherit from other classes.\n    - Classes and functions can invoke others (invocations in a class's `__init__` are attributed to the class).\nEntity ID:\n* Unique identifier including file path and module path.\n* Here's an example of an Entity ID: `\"interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func\"` identifies function `inner_func` within `method_a` of class `C` in `\"interface/C.py\"`.\n\nNotes:\n* Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n* Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n\n\nExample Usage:\n1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n        direction='downstream',\n        traversal_depth=2,\n        dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n\n2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n        direction='upstream',\n        traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n3. Exploring Repository Structure:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n      start_entities=['/'],\n      traversal_depth=2,\n      dependency_type_filter=['contains']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the tree repository structure from the root directory (/), traversing up to two levels deep and focusing only on 'contains' relationship.\n4. Generate Class Diagrams:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=selected_entity_ids,\n        direction='both',\n        traverse_depth=-1,\n        dependency_type_filter=['inherits']\n    )\n    ```\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"start_entities": {"description": "List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\nEntities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\" (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\nFor files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).", "type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "direction": {"description": "Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are: `upstream`, `downstream`, `both`.\n- 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n- 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others (how others depend on them).\n- 'both': Traversal on both direction.", "type": "string", "enum": ["upstream", "downstream", "both"], "default": "downstream"}, "traversal_depth": {"description": "Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).", "type": "integer", "default": 2}, "entity_type_filter": {"description": "List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal. If None, all entity types are included.", "type": ["array", "null"], "items": {"type": "string"}, "default": null}, "dependency_type_filter": {"description": "List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal. If None, all dependency types are included.", "type": ["array", "null"], "items": {"type": "string"}, "default": null}}, "required": ["start_entities"]}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 98082, "completion_tokens": 508}, "time": 124.23105096817017}]}}
{"instance_id": "pandas-dev__pandas-60277", "found_files": [["pandas/core/generic.py", "pandas/io/formats/excel.py", "pandas/io/excel/_openpyxl.py", "pandas/io/excel/_xlsxwriter.py"]], "found_modules": [["pandas/core/generic.py:NDFrame", "pandas/core/indexes/interval.py:IntervalIndex", "pandas/io/formats/excel.py:ExcelFormatter", "pandas/core/strings/object_array.py:ObjectStringArrayMixin", "pandas/io/formats/format.py:DataFrameFormatter", "pandas/core/indexes/range.py:RangeIndex", "asv_bench/benchmarks/frame_methods.py:Count", "pandas/io/excel/_openpyxl.py:OpenpyxlWriter", "pandas/io/excel/_xlsxwriter.py:XlsxWriter"]], "found_entities": [["pandas/core/generic.py:NDFrame.to_excel", "pandas/core/indexes/interval.py:IntervalIndex._index_as_unique", "pandas/io/formats/excel.py:ExcelFormatter._format_header_mi", "pandas/io/formats/excel.py:ExcelFormatter._format_hierarchical_rows", "pandas/core/strings/object_array.py:ObjectStringArrayMixin._str_removeprefix", "pandas/io/formats/format.py:DataFrameFormatter.has_index_names", "pandas/core/indexes/range.py:RangeIndex.__iter__", "asv_bench/benchmarks/frame_methods.py:Count.time_count_mixed_dtypes", "pandas/io/formats/format.py:DataFrameFormatter.has_column_names", "pandas/io/excel/_openpyxl.py:OpenpyxlWriter._write_cells", "pandas/io/excel/_xlsxwriter.py:XlsxWriter._write_cells"]], "raw_output_loc": ["Based on my analysis, I can now provide the locations that need documentation updates for the `merge_cells=\"columns\"` feature. Here's the result in the requested format:\n\n```\npandas/core/generic.py\nline: 2164\nline: 2214-2215\nfunction: NDFrame.to_excel\n\npandas/io/formats/excel.py\nline: 633-634\nline: 803-805\nclass: ExcelFormatter\nfunction: _format_header_mi\nfunction: _format_hierarchical_rows\n\npandas/io/excel/_openpyxl.py\nline: 507-513\nclass: OpenpyxlWriter\nfunction: _write_cells\n\npandas/io/excel/_xlsxwriter.py\nline: 507-513\nclass: XlsxWriter\nfunction: _write_cells\n\npandas/io/forms/excel.py\nline: 785-864\nclass: ExcelFormatter\nfunction: _format_hierarchical_rows\n```\n\nThese locations are ordered by importance:\n\n1. `pandas/core/generic.py` is the main implementation file where the `merge_cells` parameter is defined in the `to_excel` method. The docstring needs to be updated here to include the new `\"columns\"` option.\n\n2. `pandas/io/formats/excel.py` contains the core logic for handling merged cells in hierarchical data. The `_format_header_mi` and `_format_hierarchical_rows` functions implement the cell merging behavior.\n\n3. `pandas/io/excel/_openpyxl.py` contains the implementation for OpenPyXL engine that handles the actual Excel file creation and merging.\n\n4. `pandas/io/excel/_xlsxwriter.py` contains the implementation for XlsxWriter engine that also supports cell merging.\n\n5. The complete hierarchy of hierarchical row formatting is shown in `pandas/io/excel/_forms.py` where the base formatting logic resides.\n\nThese files together implement the `merge_cells` functionality that supports three modes:\n- `True`: default behavior of merging MultiIndex and Hierarchical Rows as merged cells\n- `\"columns\"`: merges only columns when there are duplicate values\n- `False`: disables merging"], "meta_data": {"repo": "pandas-dev/pandas", "base_commit": "4fcee0e431135bf6fa97440d4d7e17a96630fe6e", "problem_statement": "DOC: Document merge_cells=\"columns\" in to_excel\nhttps://pandas.pydata.org/docs/dev/reference/api/pandas.DataFrame.to_excel.html\r\n\r\nThe `merge_cells` argument can also take `\"columns\"` due to #35384. This should be added to the docstring.\n", "patch": "diff --git a/pandas/core/generic.py b/pandas/core/generic.py\nindex 35014674565ff..3a83a3997f881 100644\n--- a/pandas/core/generic.py\n+++ b/pandas/core/generic.py\n@@ -2211,8 +2211,9 @@ def to_excel(\n             via the options ``io.excel.xlsx.writer`` or\n             ``io.excel.xlsm.writer``.\n \n-        merge_cells : bool, default True\n-            Write MultiIndex and Hierarchical Rows as merged cells.\n+        merge_cells : bool or 'columns', default False\n+            If True, write MultiIndex index and columns as merged cells.\n+            If 'columns', merge MultiIndex column cells only.\n         {encoding_parameter}\n         inf_rep : str, default 'inf'\n             Representation for infinity (there is no native representation for\n"}, "usage": {"cost($)": "0", "prompt_tokens": 57409, "completion_tokens": 702}, "loc_trajs": {"trajs": [{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "A chat between a curious user and an artificial intelligence assistant. The assistant gives helpful, detailed answers to the user's questions.\nThe assistant can use a Python environment with <execute_ipython>, e.g.:\n<execute_ipython>\nprint(\"Hello World!\")\n</execute_ipython>\n\nApart from the standard Python library, the assistant can also use the following functions (already imported) in <execute_ipython> environment:\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms: Optional[List[str]] = None, line_nums: Optional[List] = None, file_path_or_pattern: Optional[str] = '**/*.py') -> str:\n    Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n    This function supports retrieving the complete content of a code entity,\n    searching for code entities such as classes or functions by keywords, or locating specific lines within a file.\n    It also supports filtering searches based on a file path or file pattern.\n    Note:\n    1. If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n    - If a term is formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') ,\n    or just 'file_path', the corresponding complete code is retrieved or file content is retrieved.\n    - If a term matches a file, class, or function name, matched entities are retrieved.\n    - If there is no match with any module name, it attempts to find code snippets that likely contain the term.\n    2. If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets at the specified lines within the file defined by\n    `file_path_or_pattern`.\n    Args:\n    search_terms (Optional[List[str]]): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase.\n    Terms can be formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' to search for a specific module or entity within a file\n    (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') or as 'file_path' to retrieve the complete content\n    of a file. This can also include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments.\n    line_nums (Optional[List[int]]): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file.\n    When provided, `file_path_or_pattern` must specify a valid file path.\n    file_path_or_pattern (Optional[str]): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results\n    to particular files or directories. Defaults to '**/*.py', meaning all Python files are searched by default.\n    If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n    Returns:\n    str: The search results, which may include code snippets, matching entities, or complete file content.\n    Example Usage:\n    # Search for the full content of a specific file\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py'])\n    # Search for a specific function\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n    # Search for specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\n    result = search_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n    # Combined search for a module name and within a specific file pattern\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"], file_path_or_pattern=\"src/**/*.py\")\n\nexplore_graph_structure(start_entities: List[str], direction: str = 'downstream', traversal_depth: int = 1, entity_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None, dependency_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None):\n    Args:\n    start_entities:\n    direction:\n    traversal_depth:\n    entity_type_filter:\n    dependency_type_filter:\n    Returns:\n\nexplore_tree_structure(start_entities: List[str], direction: str = 'downstream', traversal_depth: int = 2, entity_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None, dependency_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None):\n    Analyzes and displays the dependency structure around specified entities in a code graph.\n    This function searches and presents relationships and dependencies for the specified entities (such as classes, functions, files, or directories) in a code graph.\n    It explores how the input entities relate to others, using defined types of dependencies, including 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes' and 'inherits'.\n    The search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n    Example Usage:\n    1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    get_local_structure(\n    start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n    direction='downstream',\n    traversal_depth=2,\n    entity_type_filter=['class', 'function'],\n    dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n    2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    get_local_structure(\n    start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n    direction='upstream',\n    traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n    Notes:\n    * Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n    * Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n    * Graph Context: The function operates on a pre-built code graph containing entities (e.g., files, classes and functions) and dependencies representing their interactions and relationships.\n    Parameters:\n    ----------\n    start_entities : list[str]\n    List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\n    - Entities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\"\n    (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\n    - For files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).\n    direction : str, optional\n    Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are:\n    - 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n    - 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others\n    (how others depend on them).\n    - 'both': Traversal in both directions.\n    Default is 'downstream'.\n    traversal_depth : int, optional\n    Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).\n    Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).\n    Default is 2.\n    entity_type_filter : list[str], optional\n    List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal.\n    If None, all entity types are included.\n    Default is None.\n    dependency_type_filter : list[str], optional\n    List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal.\n    If None, all dependency types are included.\n    Default is None.\n    Returns:\n    -------\n    result : object\n    An object representing the traversal results, which includes discovered entities and their dependencies.\n\n\nFunction Usage Guidelines:\n- Issue Analysis and Retrieval: Begin by analyzing the issue and extracting relevant information for retrieval. Use the functions `search_in_repo` to gather more insights about the issue.\n- Accessing Files and Code: Use `get_file_structures` or `search_class_structures` to gain an overview of the files or classes; Call `get_file_content` to access the content of specific file relevant to the issue, and employ `search_class` or `search_method` to retrieve specific modules when you know the specific names of these modules.\n\n- Comprehensive Dependency Analysis: Prioritize analyzing the code dependencies of functions, methods, or classes mentioned in the problem description or those identified in relevant locations. Use `search_invoke_and_reference` as the primary tool to ensure all interactions within the codebase are captured.\n\n- Repository Overview: Use `get_repo_structure` to gain an overview of the repository. This aids in independently searching for related files or analyzing results from previous steps. However, this tool is relatively expensive, use other tools to search files, classes or methods if you know their potential names.\n- File Limitations: You may search as many files as necessary, but make sure not include noise.\n- Exclusions: Do not include any existing test case files in your findings.\n- Evaluate and prioritize findings from different search methods, reranking the answer when you give final output.\nIMPORTANT:\n- Parameter Accuracy: Ensure that the parameters used for each tool call are accurate, avoiding assumptions.\n\nResponses should be concise.\nThe assistant should attempt fewer things at a time instead of putting too many commands OR too much code in one \"execute\" block.\nInclude ONLY ONE <execute_ipython> per response, unless the assistant is finished with the task or needs more input or action from the user in order to proceed.\nIf the assistant is finished with the task you MUST include <finish></finish> in your response.\nIMPORTANT: Execute code using <execute_ipython> whenever possible.\nThe assistant should utilize full file paths to prevent path-related errors.\nThe assistant must avoid apologies and thanks in its responses.\nYou have access to the following functions:\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #1: finish ----\nDescription: Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task.\nNo parameters are required for this function.\n---- END FUNCTION #1 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #2: search_code_snippets ----\nDescription: Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n\nParameters:\n  (1) search_terms (array, optional): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (2) line_nums (array, optional): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (3) file_path_or_pattern (string, optional): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n---- END FUNCTION #2 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #3: get_entity_contents ----\nDescription: \nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n\nParameters:\n  (1) entity_names (array, required): A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n---- END FUNCTION #3 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #4: explore_tree_structure ----\nDescription: \nUnified repository exploring tool that traverses a pre-built code graph to retrieve dependency structure around specified entities.\nThe search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n\nCode Graph Definition:\n* Entity Types: 'directory', 'file', 'class', 'function'.\n* Dependency Types: 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits'.\n* Hierarchy:\n    - Directories contain files and subdirectories.\n    - Files contain classes and functions.\n    - Classes contain inner classes and methods.\n    - Functions can contain inner functions.\n* Interactions:\n    - Files/classes/functions can import classes and functions.\n    - Classes can inherit from other classes.\n    - Classes and functions can invoke others (invocations in a class's `__init__` are attributed to the class).\nEntity ID:\n* Unique identifier including file path and module path.\n* Here's an example of an Entity ID: `\"interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func\"` identifies function `inner_func` within `method_a` of class `C` in `\"interface/C.py\"`.\n\nNotes:\n* Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n* Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n\n\nExample Usage:\n1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n        direction='downstream',\n        traversal_depth=2,\n        dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n\n2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n        direction='upstream',\n        traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n3. Exploring Repository Structure:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n      start_entities=['/'],\n      traversal_depth=2,\n      dependency_type_filter=['contains']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the tree repository structure from the root directory (/), traversing up to two levels deep and focusing only on 'contains' relationship.\n4. Generate Class Diagrams:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=selected_entity_ids,\n        direction='both',\n        traverse_depth=-1,\n        dependency_type_filter=['inherits']\n    )\n    ```\n\nParameters:\n  (1) start_entities (array, required): List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\nEntities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\" (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\nFor files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).\n  (2) direction (string, optional): Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are: `upstream`, `downstream`, `both`.\n- 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n- 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others (how others depend on them).\n- 'both': Traversal on both direction.\nAllowed values: [`upstream`, `downstream`, `both`]\n  (3) traversal_depth (integer, optional): Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).\n  (4) entity_type_filter (['array', 'null'], optional): List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal. If None, all entity types are included.\n  (5) dependency_type_filter (['array', 'null'], optional): List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal. If None, all dependency types are included.\n---- END FUNCTION #4 ----\n\n\nIf you choose to call a function ONLY reply in the following format with NO suffix:\n\n<function=example_function_name>\n<parameter=example_parameter_1>value_1</parameter>\n<parameter=example_parameter_2>\nThis is the value for the second parameter\nthat can span\nmultiple lines\n</parameter>\n</function>\n\n<IMPORTANT>\nReminder:\n- Function calls MUST follow the specified format, start with <function= and end with </function>\n- Required parameters MUST be specified\n- Only call one function at a time\n- You may provide optional reasoning for your function call in natural language BEFORE the function call, but NOT after.\n- If there is no function call available, answer the question like normal with your current knowledge and do not tell the user about function calls\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "\nGiven the following GitHub issue description, your objective is to localize the specific files, classes or functions, and lines of code that are relevant to the solution for the issue. More specifically, these issues are all \"feature request\" issues, which means that you need to find the locations the feature described in the issue.\n\nFollow these steps to localize the issue:\n## Step 1: Categorize and Extract Key Problem Information\n - Classify the issue description into the following categories:\n    Requested feature description, behavior with out this feature, expected behavior with the feature, and additional context.\n - Identify modules in the 'pandas-dev' package mentioned in each category.\n - Use extracted keywords and line numbers to search for relevant code references for additional context.\n\n## Step 2: Locate Referenced Modules\n- Accurately determine specific modules\n    - Explore the repo to familiarize yourself with its structure.\n    - Analyze the described workflow to identify specific modules or components being referenced.\n- Pay special attention to distinguishing between modules with similar names using context and described workflow.\n- Output Format for collected relevant modules:\n    - Use the format: 'file_path:QualifiedName'\n    - E.g., for a function `calculate_sum` in the `MathUtils` class located in `src/helpers/math_helpers.py`, represent it as: 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'.\n\n## Step 3: Analyze the feature\n- Understand the Purpose of the Feature in the Context of the Codebase\n    - Understand what the feature is supposed to do.\n    - Understand why it is needed.\n    - Understand how it fits into the overall functionality of the codebase.\n- Analyze the Feature's Relationship with the Codebase\n    - Identify the key affected components of the feature.\n    - Understand how these components interact with each other and with existing code.\n    - Consider the inputs, outputs, and side effects of the feature.\n\n## Step 4: Locate Areas for Feature Implementation\n- Locate specific files, functions, or lines of code implementing the key part of the requested feature or containing critical information for resolving the issue.\n- Consider upstream and downstream dependencies that may affect or be affected by the issue.\n- Think Thoroughly: List multiple potential solutions and consider edge cases that could impact the resolution.\n\n## Output Format for Final Results:\nYour final output should list the locations requiring modification, wrapped with triple backticks ```\nEach location should include the file path, class name (if applicable), function name, or line numbers, ordered by importance.\nYour answer would better include about 5 files.\n\nIMPORTANT FORMAT RULES:\n- Output ONLY the location block, followed by `<finish></finish>`.\n- Do NOT include explanations, bullets, headings, numbering, summaries, or notes before or after the location block.\n- Each file path MUST appear on its own line and end with `.py`.\n- Do NOT write `file.py:QualifiedName` on a single line in the final answer.\n- After a file path line, use separate `line:`, `class:`, and `function:` lines as needed.\n\n### Examples:\n```\nfull_path1/file1.py\nline: 10\nclass: MyClass1\nfunction: my_function1\n\nfull_path2/file2.py\nline: 76\nfunction: MyClass2.my_function2\n\nfull_path3/file3.py\nline: 24\nline: 156\nfunction: my_function3\n```\n\nReturn just the location(s)\n\nNote: Your thinking should be thorough and so it's fine if it's very long.\n\n--- BEGIN PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\nTitle: DOC: Document merge_cells=\"columns\" in to_excel\n\nhttps://pandas.pydata.org/docs/dev/reference/api/pandas.DataFrame.to_excel.html\r\n\r\nThe `merge_cells` argument can also take `\"columns\"` due to #35384. This should be added to the docstring.\n--- END PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\n\nIMPORTANT: You should ONLY interact with the environment provided to you AND NEVER ASK FOR HUMAN HELP.\nDon't include any lambda functions!\nYou should NOT modify any files!\n\n\nSupplemental context information:\n\nYou will receive supplemental context for the current task. Use it to better understand the issue and to localize the relevant code more accurately.\n\nTreat this context as additional evidence, not as the final answer.\n- It may contain noise.\n- It may be incomplete.\n- You should carefully analyze it and decide how to use it when searching for relevant code locations.\n\nDepending on the settings, the supplemental context may include:\n1. Unique artifacts\n2. Unique dependency tree\n\n## How to use this supplemental context\n- Use it to identify candidate files, classes, functions, and execution paths that may be relevant to the issue.\n- Cross-check it against the issue description and the repository evidence you collect.\n- Do not assume every artifact is important.\n- Do not assume the provided context is sufficient by itself.\n\n## What the provided information means\nAssume the issue sent to you is a feature request requesting feature F.\n\n1. Unique artifacts\n- The unique artifacts are obtained through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis.\n- For the requested feature described in the issue text, two Python scripts, A.py and B.py, describing the same process are executed and traced.\n- The only difference is that A.py is executed with feature F enabled, while B.py is executed with the feature disabled.\n- By comparing the two tracing results, the overlapping part between the trace for A.py and B.py is subtracted.\n- The remaining artifacts are the unique artifacts that appear only in A.py.\n- These unique artifacts are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n\n2. Unique dependency tree\n- The unique dependency tree is obtained by further analyzing the data dependencies of the unique artifacts.\n- It contains the dataflows associated with the unique artifacts.\n- It can help you understand how the unique artifacts are related to each other and how they are related to other parts of the codebase.\n\n\nHere is the information about the current task:\n1. Unique artifacts (obtained through Software Reconnaissance-style analysis):\n# Unique Artifacts\n\nUse these unique artifacts as supplemental hints, not as final answers.\n\n## Summary\n- Trace A role: `feature_positive`\n- Trace B role: `feature_negative`\n- Comparison: `trace_a_minus_trace_b`\n- Unique functions in JSON: `1`\n- Unique functions shown here: `0`\n- Target package filter: `pandas`\n\n## Artifact Candidates\n- No unique functions matched the current target-package filter.\n\n2. Unique dependency tree (dataflows associated with unique artifacts):\n# Unique Dependency Hints\n\nUse these dependency hints as supplemental evidence, not as final answers.\n\n## Summary\n- Trace started at: `2026-03-30 15:14:52`\n- Retained files: `6`\n- Retained symbols: `28`\n- Retained edges: `29`\n- Matched dependency paths: `80`\n- Named locations are limited to the target package: `pandas`\n- External dependencies are summarized generically and are not listed by file or function name.\n\n## Related File Clusters\n- `pandas/core/indexes/multi.py`: appears in `14` retained dependency path(s)\n- `pandas/core/generic.py`: appears in `10` retained dependency path(s)\n- `pandas/core/arrays/categorical.py`: appears in `6` retained dependency path(s)\n- `pandas/io/formats/excel.py`: appears in `5` retained d